  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nHigh pressure continues to nudge in keeping our weather dry,\nalbeit cloudy today, especially across the mountains where low\nclouds will linger through Sunday morning. High temperatures\nwill be below normal today with the lowlands staying in the 40s\nto low 50s. The mountains will remain in the 30s and 40s. A\nwarming trend starts tomorrow with 50s and 60s arriving across \nthe area. This warming trend will continue through at least mid\nweek.\n\nOvernight lows will be quite chilly and below normal the next\nfew nights, especially tonight into Sunday morning. Lows tonight\nare projected to drop into the mid 20s to the mid 30s. Hard\nfreeze and widespread frost is anticipated Sunday morning and\nagain Monday morning, possibly even Tuesday morning due to these\ntemperatures. As such, a freeze warning is in effect across our\nactive growing season counties from midnight tonight until 10 AM\nSunday morning. Additional headlines are possible for Monday\nmorning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDry weather is expected to last through at least Wednesday. A\ncold front moves into the area and stalls Wednesday night into\nThursday. A large, amplified, upper-level disturbance moves\nthrough on Friday after small shortwave disturbances cross.\nThese features are projected to bring active weather Thursday\nthrough Saturday. Mostly all rain is expected throughout the\nevent, but some rain/snow mix maybe possible at times across \nthe mountains Thursday night through Friday as colder\ntemperatures move in. Models still diverge on a solution this\nfar out so confidence is low.\n\nStrong, blustery northwesterly flow and subsequent colder air \nwill move into the area Friday and Saturday with temperatures\nprojected to be much below normal for next weekend.",
